{"result": "NUTRICIÓN PARENTERAL, or parenteral nutrition (PN), is a specialized medical practice that involves the intravenous delivery of essential nutrients directly into the bloodstream, bypassing the gastrointestinal tract. This method is crucial for patients who are unable to receive nutrition through traditional means, such as oral or enteral feeding, particularly in cases where the digestive system is non-functional or when enteral nutrition is insufficient to meet the patient's caloric and protein needs.\n\nParenteral nutrition is indicated for various patient populations, including those with severe malnutrition, intestinal failure, pancreatitis, inflammatory bowel disease, and certain cancers. It is often employed in critical care settings, pediatric patients, and individuals undergoing treatments like chemotherapy or surgery, where maintaining adequate nutritional status is essential. The administration of parenteral nutrition requires careful monitoring and management to avoid complications, which can include infections, metabolic imbalances, and gastrointestinal issues.\n\nTypically prepared in a pharmacy to ensure asepsis and stability of the components, parenteral nutrition is administered to patients with central venous access and is part of fluid and electrolyte management. While it serves as a vital alternative when enteral feeding is not feasible, it is generally recommended to be used in conjunction with oral nutrition whenever possible to reduce the risk of complications. Parenteral nutrition is particularly important for patients who cannot tolerate enteral nutrition after surgery or in cases where enteral nutrition is contraindicated or insufficient.\n\nIn summary, NUTRICIÓN PARENTERAL is an essential method of providing macro and micronutrients intravenously to patients who cannot consume food orally or absorb nutrients through their digestive system, ensuring their nutritional needs are met in critical situations.", "input": "\nYou are a he